    bodyFingerprintText: "What Are PCOS Symptoms If My Periods Are Regular?\nRegular periods do not rule out PCOS (also called PMOS after the 2026 rename). Some people still qualify through androgen-related skin or hair changes plus ovarian findings even when bleeds look clockwork, and some “regular” bleeds still hide irregular ovulation. Acne, hirsutism or body size alone cannot diagnose the condition. Assessment is appropriate when features cluster or progress, after other causes are considered, not from a single symptom such as acne.\nCan you have PCOS/PMOS with regular periods?\nYes. Under the 2023 international guideline, adults can meet criteria without irregular cycles if androgen excess and an ovarian marker are both present after other causes are excluded. That is why tidy calendars do not close the question. Our what-is-PCOS guide explains the full diagnostic criteria.\nBleeding on hormonal contraception is not the same as a natural ovulatory cycle. Pills, injections, implants and hormonal intrauterine systems can create scheduled withdrawal bleeds or spotting that mask underlying irregularity. If symptoms began before contraception, or persist when you are not on hormones, say so—otherwise a tidy calendar can give false reassurance.\nRegular-looking bleeds also do not automatically prove monthly ovulation. If fertility is on your mind, ask specifically about ovulatory function rather than assuming the calendar answers that question.\nWhich skin, hair and metabolic clues are worth discussing?\nSkin and hair changes that often enter the conversation include acne that remains stubborn beyond adolescence, coarse hair on the chin, upper lip, chest or abdomen that is new or increasing, and thinning at the scalp crown or temples. These signs can have other causes, including thyroid disease, medicines, or hereditary patterning. They are discussion points, not automatic proof of PCOS/PMOS.\nDo not dismiss longstanding hirsutism as “just family hair.” Duration alone does not make assessment pointless—especially when threading or shaving frequency has climbed, or when hair growth affects work, intimacy or mood. Describe your own baseline and how much effort removal now takes.\nMetabolic themes—family history of diabetes, dark velvety skin patches in body folds, or marked energy crashes after meals—may prompt broader health screening. Body size is not the disease and not the test: people in smaller bodies can meet criteria, and people in larger bodies can have acne or cycle issues for other reasons.\nWhen assessment may help—and what does not diagnose you\nConsider a gynaecology review if androgen-type skin or hair changes are progressing, if you have metabolic risk factors you want checked in context, if fertility planning feels stuck despite tidy cycles, or if you simply need a structured answer rather than continued uncertainty. One late period, one chin hair, or a single photo comparison cannot confirm PCOS/PMOS.\nUltrasound findings of multifollicular ovaries are common in young adults and need age and clinical context. A normal scan does not erase symptoms that still deserve care. Self-ordered hormone panels without a clinician’s question often create more anxiety than clarity.\nIf irregular bleeding is actually your main problem, see our guide on why periods can be irregular so every untidy calendar is not forced into a PCOS frame before examination. Dr. Charmi Shah interprets pattern, life stage, contraception status and examination together.\nRapid progression versus longstanding change\nLongstanding, slowly evolving hirsutism or adult acne still warrants a calm diagnostic conversation when it bothers you or clusters with other features. Gradual change over years is not a reason to minimise symptoms.\nRapid progression or virilisation is different. Deepening voice, clitoral enlargement, or abruptly accelerating coarse hair growth needs prompt medical assessment to look for other androgen-excess causes, not a wait-and-see approach based on a regular period calendar.\nBring a short symptom timeline: when skin or hair changed, contraception dates, medicines and supplements, and your top questions. Photos of skin are optional aids only if you are comfortable sharing them.\nWhen to seek urgent care\nSeek urgent or emergency care for abrupt severe pelvic pain, fainting during a heavy bleed, soaking through protection with dizziness, fever with pelvic pain, or a positive pregnancy test with pain or bleeding. Those problems need prompt triage, not another PCOS symptom article.\nRoutine booking remains appropriate for progressive hirsutism, stubborn acne with cycle questions, or a planned diagnostic review. Separating urgent pathways from elective assessment keeps both safer."
