    bodyFingerprintText: "Why Is My Period Late If My Pregnancy Test Is Negative?\nA late period with a negative home pregnancy test can still mean pregnancy if you tested too early or did not follow the instructions, because negative results are less reliable than positive ones. NHS advice is that most tests are most reliable from the first day of a missed period, or at least 21 days after unprotected sex if you do not know when the next period is due. Other common reasons for delay include stress, contraception effects, breastfeeding, weight or training shifts, thyroid problems, approaching menopause, and PCOS/PMOS. Persistent missed periods need clinical review; urgent pain or bleeding needs emergency care even if today’s stick is negative.\nWhat a negative home test can and cannot show\nHome tests detect the pregnancy hormone hCG in urine. NHS explains that a positive result is almost certainly correct, while a negative result is less reliable—especially if instructions were not followed or the test was taken too early. Some medicines can affect results. If you still think you might be pregnant after a negative result, wait a few days and repeat the test; speak to a GP if a second test is negative but the period has still not arrived.\nHormonal contraception does not stop a pregnancy test from working. No method is completely effective, so testing remains appropriate when pregnancy is possible. Read the packet timing carefully; very sensitive kits sometimes claim earlier use, but the NHS public rule of thumb remains the missed-period day for most kits.\nOther reasons periods run late\nNHS missed-period pages list pregnancy first, then stress, perimenopause (often between about 45 and 55), polycystic ovary syndrome now known as polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome, sudden weight loss, being overweight, very high exercise load, hormonal contraception such as the pill, injection or intrauterine system, and breastfeeding. Medical conditions including thyroid disorders can also stop or delay bleeds.\nOur guide to why periods become irregular explains how to describe a changing pattern over months. This page answers the immediate puzzle of a late bleed plus a negative stick, not the full differential of chronic irregularity.\nWhen to repeat the test and when to seek review\nIf your period is only a few days late and the first test was early, retest after a short wait or from the day your period was due, using first-morning urine if the packet suggests it. Keep the used kit or a photo of the result window if you will discuss it in clinic.\nUK NHS advice on periods and missed periods suggests seeing a doctor if you have missed three consecutive periods after negative testing, among other prompts. Also seek review sooner if you have associated symptoms such as marked weight change, unwanted facial hair, extreme tiredness, or if cycles have become newly irregular. Thresholds and access routes differ by country; treat the three-missed-period prompt as NHS wording when you use it.\nUrgent symptoms—do not wait on another home test\nNHS ectopic-pregnancy advice says to seek help for a combination of symptoms if you might be pregnant—even without a positive test. Warning features include one-sided lower tummy pain, vaginal bleeding or brown watery discharge, shoulder-tip pain, and discomfort when peeing or pooing. Emergency care is needed for sudden intense tummy pain with dizziness, fainting, looking very pale or feeling very sick—signs that a tube rupture may be occurring.\nIn India, call emergency services on 112 for those acute features rather than messaging a routine booking line. A negative stick does not rule out an early pregnancy complication. Dr. Charmi Shah can help with non-urgent delayed periods once pregnancy emergencies are excluded."
